        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(openai): add handshakeTimeout to realtime voice WebSocket",
        "body": "AI-assisted: yes. I reviewed and understand the change.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe OpenAI realtime voice WebSocket creates `new WebSocket()` without `handshakeTimeout`. The `ws` library defaults this to 0 (unlimited).\n\nThe bridge-level `connectTimeout` watchdog (`setTimeout → ws.terminate()`) catches stalls AFTER `open` fires (session not configured), but does NOT protect against a TCP peer that accepts the connection and stalls the HTTP upgrade — `ws` never emits `open` in that case, and `ws.terminate()` on a CONNECTING socket may not clean up underlying TCP resources. The `handshakeTimeout` operates at the ws library level and directly bounds the upgrade handshake.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdd `handshakeTimeout: OpenAIRealtimeVoiceBridge.CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS` (10 s) to the WebSocket options, matching Alix-007's pattern in Slack, Discord, Mattermost, xAI, and Signal WebSockets.\n\n## User Impact\n\nStalled OpenAI realtime voice connections now fail within 10 s during the WebSocket upgrade phase. Healthy connections are unaffected.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Unit tests\n\n- `openai/realtime-voice-provider.test.ts`: **66/66 passed**.\n- `expect(options?.handshakeTimeout).toBe(10_000)` — verifies the constant reaches the constructor.\n\n### ws library contract proof\n\n```json\n{\"handshakeTimeout_ms\":2000,\"error\":\"Opening handshake has timed out\",\"elapsed_ms\":2011,\"result\":\"TIMED_OUT\"}\n{\"handshakeTimeout_ms\":10000,\"error\":\"Opening handshake has timed out\",\"elapsed_ms\":10012,\"result\":\"TIMED_OUT\"}\n```\n\nThe production value (10 s) causes the ws library to reject a stalled upgrade within the deadline. The pre-fix default (0) waits indefinitely.\n\n### handshakeTimeout vs bridge watchdog (non-redundant)\n\n| Layer | Guards | Failure mode |\n|------|------|------|\n| `handshakeTimeout` (ws) | TCP accept → HTTP upgrade | Stalled before `open` event |\n| `connectTimeout` (bridge) | WS open → session configured | Stalled after `open` event |\n\nThese protect different phases. The bridge watchdog fires `setTimeout(() => ws.terminate(), 10_000)`, but `ws.terminate()` on a socket still in CONNECTING state (stalled upgrade) may not release the underlying TCP fd — the ws library must handle this internally via `handshakeTimeout`.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
